abstract: "Loss or distortion of taste is a recognised accompaniment of viral infections and was reported very widely during the COVID-19 pandemic. This Cochrane-level systematic review asked, for new-onset taste dysfunction attributed to a confirmed, probable or possible viral infection: which viruses are associated, with what frequency, timing and recovery (Q1); which pathogenesis mechanisms have been proposed, and how direct the evidence for each is (Q2); and what interventions have been studied, with what effects, doses, harms and special-population considerations (Q3). PubMed, Europe PMC and ClinicalTrials.gov were searched in full on 20 November 2026; 5,988 records were retrieved, 3,501 screened, and 363 studies included (362 unique publications). Acute taste dysfunction in SARS-CoV-2 pooled at 37.7% (95% CI 32.9 to 42.7; 137 studies, 155,010 participants; I squared 99.5%; 95% prediction interval 5.3 to 86.7%). Complete recovery pooled at 87.4% within 4 weeks, 85.2% at more than 4 to 12 weeks and 87.8% beyond 12 weeks, each from a different study set. Only 23 studies tested any intervention; no treatment has the comparative evidence to support a recommendation. GRADE certainty was very low for most outcomes. All pooled evidence concerns SARS-CoV-2."
